As posted previously, the output of the python tool chardet said that the file _nInMar2MichaelDay.txt was “UTF-8-SIG”. The official Python documentation explains:

> "Microsoft invented a variant of UTF-8 (that Python 2.5 calls "utf-8-sig") for its Notepad program: Before any of the Unicode characters is written to the file, a UTF-8 encoded BOM (which looks like this as a byte sequence: 0xef, 0xbb, 0xbf) is written."
 https://docs.python.org/2/library/codecs.html

In the past, I’ve used command-line tools to remove BOMs — don’t think R can do this. Pretty sure I used sed (available in cygwin on Windows). Also, iconv may just remove the BOM if you convert to UTF-8.
https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1068650/using-awk-to-remove-the-byte-order-mark#3622153

